UID:nasa-space-roboticist-challenge-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260924
SUMMARY:Deadline: NASA Space Roboticist Challenge: on-orbit robotic arm exp
 eriment time (US researchers)
DESCRIPTION:NASA is opening access to the robotic arm of the Fly Foundation
 al Robots (FFR) mission\, a seven-degree-of-freedom arm in low Earth orbit
 \, to a select group of US researchers with a compelling experiment. WHAT 
 YOU SUBMIT: a white paper proposing a short\, focused experiment using the
  FFR robotic arm. Up to 15 teams advance to Phase 2\, where invited partic
 ipants conduct simulation and validation testing\, including site visits t
 o Goddard Space Flight Center\; teams that pass validation receive an offe
 r of on-orbit experiment time on the FFR mission. WHO CAN APPLY: US resear
 chers only\, including principal investigators\, post-doctoral researchers
 \, professors and highly qualified graduate students\; eligibility documen
 tation must be submitted at registration. TIMELINE: Phase 0 eligibility re
 gistration opened 20 May 2026 and closes 23 September 2026 at 12:59 PM ET\
 ; Phase 1 white papers close 2 October 2026 at 12:59 PM ET. Register and a
 pply via the challenge portal at spaceroboticistchallenge.com. Note that t
 he award is experiment time and mission support rather than funding\, so t
 his suits researchers who can cover their own time but could never otherwi
 se access flight hardware in orbit.\n\nOrganisation: NASA\nLocation: Remot
 e participation with site visits to Goddard Space Flight Center for Phase 
 2 validation\; the experiment runs on the FFR robotic arm in low Earth orb
 it. US researchers only: eligibility documentation is required at registra
 tion.\nAward: FLAG\, IN-KIND AWARD: no cash prize. Teams that pass validat
 ion receive an offer of on-orbit experiment time on the seven-degree-of-fr
 eedom robotic arm of the Fly Foundational Robots (FFR) mission in low Eart
 h orbit\, with simulation and validation support including site visits to 
 Goddard Space Flight Center. Free to apply.\nDuration: Phase 0 registratio
 n through 23 September 2026\; Phase 1 white papers due 2 October 2026\; Ph
 ase 2 simulation and validation for invited teams\n\nMore: https://www.nas
 a.gov/directorates/stmd/prizes-challenges-crowdsourcing-program/center-of-
 excellence-for-collaborative-innovation-coeci/nasa-space-roboticist-challe
 nge/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/stmd/prizes-challenges-crowdsourcing-
 program/center-of-excellence-for-collaborative-innovation-coeci/nasa-space
 -roboticist-challenge/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Remote participation with site visits to Goddard Space Flight Cent
 er for Phase 2 validation\; the experiment runs on the FFR robotic arm in 
 low Earth orbit. US researchers only: eligibility documentation is require
 d at registration.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:trusted-creator-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trusted Creator Fellowship 2026 (News Creator Corps)
DESCRIPTION:A short-term virtual fellowship from News Creator Corps that tr
 ains independent content creators in the tactical craft of journalism: fac
 t-checking\, sourcing and interviewing\, applied directly to their own cha
 nnels during the programme so the community they already serve gets more c
 redible information. WHO IT IS FOR: independent creators with an engaged f
 ollowing on digital platforms (Instagram\, TikTok\, Twitch\, Substack\, Yo
 uTube and similar) whose content is information-based and serves a specifi
 c community. It is explicitly not aimed at traditional journalists already
  trained in reporting fundamentals. STUDENT SEATS: three places in the aut
 umn cohort are reserved for full-time college students aged 25 or younger\
 , who get the same fellowship experience as everyone else. WHAT FELLOWS RE
 CEIVE: a $5\,000 stipend on completion\, weekly virtual sessions\, and acc
 ess to discussion forums with the cohort\, trainers\, staff and alumni. EL
 IGIBILITY: US-based only. KEY DATES: applications close 17 August 2026 at 
 11:59pm ET\; notifications by the end of September 2026\; programme runs r
 oughly 5 October to 18 December 2026. Contact: creatorprogram@newscreatorc
 orps.org. Sibling programme to the Rural Creator Fellowship.\n\nOrganisati
 on: News Creator Corps (a project of the Good Information Foundation)\nLoc
 ation: Virtual\, meeting once a week. RESTRICTED: US-based creators only.\
 nAward: $5\,000 stipend on completing the programme. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Roughly 5 October to 18 December 2026\, virtual sessions once a 
 week\n\nMore: https://newscreatorcorps.org/programs/trusted-creator/apply/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newscreatorcorps.org/programs/trusted-creator/apply/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Virtual\, meeting once a week. RESTRICTED: US-based creators only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dedalus-foundation-senior-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Dedalus Foundation Senior Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Dedalus Foundation\, established by artist Robert Motherwel
 l\, awards a Senior Fellowship supporting critical and historical studies 
 related to painting\, sculpture\, and allied arts of the twentieth century
 . The fellowship is open to art historians\, critics\, curators\, and othe
 r writers and scholars who have demonstrated their abilities through previ
 ous accomplishments. Awards provide a stipend of up to USD 30\,000 for pro
 jects lasting up to one year\, with the amount varying by project needs. A
 pplicants must be U.S. citizens and may not be currently pursuing academic
  degrees. Applications are submitted online and reference letters through 
 a separate form. A distinguished scholar jury selects recipients\, with aw
 ards announced by mid-December.\n\nOrganisation: The Dedalus Foundation\nL
 ocation: United States (remote\, project-based)\nAward: Stipend up to USD 
 30\,000\nDuration: Up to one year\n\nMore: https://dedalusfoundation.org/p
 rograms/fellowships-awards/view/senior-fellowship/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://dedalusfoundation.org/programs/fellowships-awards/view/senior-f
 ellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (remote\, project-based)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:justice-joy-summer-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260709
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260710
SUMMARY:Deadline: Justice + Joy Summer Research Fellowship 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Justice + Joy Research Fellowship is a seven-week virtual s
 ummer program for girls\, young women\, and gender-expansive young people 
 of color (ages 18-26) in the United States who want to explore issues impa
 cting their lives\, communities\, and futures. Fellows identify a topic th
 ey care about\, such as mental health\, education\, reproductive justice\,
  immigration\, AI and technology\, gender-based violence\, beauty standard
 s\, young parenthood\, or another issue affecting their communities\, and 
 develop an original research project grounded in their experiences\, quest
 ions\, and ideas. Throughout the summer\, fellows participate in weekly vi
 rtual sessions\, engage with guest speakers\, build community with peers f
 rom across the country\, and receive mentorship and support as they develo
 p their research. The fellowship culminates in a capstone showcase where f
 ellows present their work to the Justice + Joy community\, including organ
 izational leaders\, partners\, and supporters. No prior research experienc
 e is required\; the program seeks young people who are curious\, committed
  to learning\, and interested in using research as a tool for understandin
 g\, advocacy\, and change. COMMITMENT: weekly virtual sessions on Wednesda
 ys 5:00-6:30 PM ET (9 July - 20 August 2026)\; approximately 2-4 hours of 
 independent work per week\; required orientation on Wednesday 9 July\; dev
 elopment of an individual research proposal or conceptual framework\; pres
 entation at the capstone showcase (tentatively in person\, mid-September 2
 026\, with all travel and lodging covered). WHAT YOU GET: a USD 2\,000 sti
 pend paid upon successful completion\; skills-based training in research m
 ethods\, social justice frameworks\, and community advocacy\; guest speake
 r sessions\; a peer community\; and a real audience for the research (the 
 Justice + Joy board and funders). Apply via the Google Form. Note: an expl
 icit application deadline is not stated\; the required orientation and pro
 gram start is 9 July 2026\, so apply before then.\n\nOrganisation: Justice
  + Joy\nLocation: United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase
  tentatively in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging cove
 red.\nAward: USD 2\,000 stipend paid upon successful program completion\, 
 plus skills-based training\, guest speaker sessions\, and peer community. 
 All travel and lodging for the in-person capstone are covered.\nDuration: 
 Seven-week virtual program\, 9 July to 20 August 2026\, with a capstone sh
 owcase mid-September 2026. Weekly Wednesday sessions 5:00-6:30 PM ET plus 
 approximately 2-4 hours of independent work per week.\n\nMore: https://doc
 s.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4g8Q_yny0aiBDb-
 qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfXN_oqSA7G7RRPjhLp-_McFix70m4
 g8Q_yny0aiBDb-qfHvw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:United States\; fully virtual program. Capstone showcase tentative
 ly in person (mid-September 2026)\, with travel and lodging covered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gmu-civic-science-fellow-science-policy-collaborative-2026@artificialno
 uveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
SUMMARY:Deadline: Civic Science Postdoctoral Fellow: Science Policy Collabo
 rative (George Mason University) 2026
DESCRIPTION:A grant-funded\, full-time Civic Science Postdoctoral Fellowshi
 p supporting the Science Policy Collaborative\, a national network that st
 rengthens the development\, sustainability and evaluation of U.S. science 
 policy programs. The fellow helps shape an emerging field at the heart of 
 a growing community of practice connecting science policy programs across 
 the country. WHAT YOU'LL DO: conduct a national needs assessment of U.S. s
 cience policy programs and publish the findings\; support the Collaborativ
 e's working groups by scoping deliverables and building partnerships to ex
 ecute them\; develop infrastructure for knowledge exchange (tools like log
 ic models and evaluation frameworks)\; connect programs with national and 
 international experts on the use of research evidence (URE) and evidence-i
 nformed policymaking\; present at partner meetings and convenings\; and re
 ceive mentorship from George Mason University\, UC Riverside and other lea
 ders of the Collaborative. WHO THEY'RE LOOKING FOR: someone with a doctora
 l degree\, relevant experience\, knowledge of science-for-policy programs\
 , and skills in network coordination\, needs assessment or program evaluat
 ion\, and writing for broad audiences (see the posting for exact criteria)
 . Fairfax\, VA (hybrid eligible)\; full-time 18-month postdoctoral appoint
 ment issued as two contracts (12 months plus 6 months)\; salary about USD 
 80\,000/year plus benefits. APPLY with a cover letter\, CV\, three referen
 ces and a writing sample written for broad audiences\; for full considerat
 ion apply by 5 July 2026 (open until filled). Questions to search coordina
 tor Natalie V Lapidot-Croitoru\, nlapidot@gmu.edu.\n\nOrganisation: George
  Mason University (with UC Riverside)\, via the Science Policy Collaborati
 ve and Civic Science Fellows network\nLocation: Fairfax\, Virginia\, USA (
 hybrid eligible).\nAward: Salary approximately USD 80\,000 per year plus b
 enefits. No application fee.\nDuration: 18-month postdoctoral appointment 
 (two contracts: 12 months + 6 months)\n\nMore: https://listings.jobs.gmu.e
 du/jobs/civic-science-fellow-science-policy-collaborative-fairfax-va-virgi
 nia-united-states-other?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://listings.jobs.gmu.edu/jobs/civic-science-fellow-science-policy-
 collaborative-fairfax-va-virginia-united-states-other?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fairfax\, Virginia\, USA (hybrid eligible).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:omi-cmdg-tech-media-ai-policy-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech\, Media & AI Policy Fellow (CMDG\, Open Markets Inst
 itute)
DESCRIPTION:A fellowship at the Center for Media & Digital Governance (CMDG
 ) at the Open Markets Institute\, a Washington-based think tank\, for earl
 y-career professionals interested in how AI is reshaping the information e
 cosystem\, how platform and media consolidation affect press freedom and d
 emocratic accountability\, and what regulatory and legal tools can address
  concentrated power over the news. The fellow contributes to research\, pu
 blications\, regulatory comments\, op-eds and testimony\; supports communi
 cations across the Center's Substack\, social media and partner outreach\;
  and shares administrative work (scheduling\, convening logistics\, organi
 zational systems). QUALIFICATIONS: strong research and writing skills\; a 
 bachelor's degree or higher in journalism\, law\, policy\, communications 
 or a related field (law\, policy\, or graduate background and prior public
 ation or journalism experience preferred)\; comfort with social media and 
 AI tools\; experience with data analysis\, regulatory agencies\, and/or po
 licy tracking\; international or non-U.S. regulatory exposure a plus. TO A
 PPLY: send a resume\, one-page cover letter\, and one writing sample to jo
 bs@openmarketsinstitute.org with the subject line 'CMDG Fellowship Applica
 tion'\; links to relevant social media or published work welcome\; no phon
 e calls. NOTE: applications are reviewed on a rolling basis\, so there is 
 no firm deadline\; the date shown is an approximate cutoff and applying ea
 rlier is advised.\n\nOrganisation: Center for Media & Digital Governance\,
  Open Markets Institute\nLocation: Washington\, D.C.\, USA\nAward: USD 20 
 to 25 per hour. Candidates who bring external funding or support that offs
 ets compensation are encouraged to note it\; total compensation may be str
 uctured accordingly.\nDuration: Fellowship (hourly)\n\nMore: https://cmdg.
 tech/tech-media-ai-policy-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://cmdg.tech/tech-media-ai-policy-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C.\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rowland-fellowship-harvard-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:Deadline: Rowland Fellowship at Harvard (incoming 2027 cohort)
DESCRIPTION:Rowland Fellowship at Harvard for outstanding early-career expe
 rimentalists in any field of science or engineering\, providing the opport
 unity to establish an independent research programme at the Rowland Instit
 ute in Cambridge\, Massachusetts. Founded by Edwin Land in 1980 to foster 
 high-risk creative research and joined to Harvard in 2002\, the Institute 
 particularly supports scholars with potential to establish ground-breaking
  research programmes that bridge traditional disciplinary boundaries. ELIG
 IBILITY: applicants should currently be completing their PhDs or have rece
 ived their PhD after 1 May 2025\; the doctoral degree must be completed pr
 ior to starting the Fellowship. Fellows may have the opportunity to teach 
 undergraduates during their Fellowship. Apply via the Harvard Careers post
 ing linked from the Rowland Institute fellowships page. Applications are c
 urrently open\; the call does not state a fixed closing date\, so check th
 e Harvard Careers posting for the current deadline.\n\nOrganisation: The R
 owland Institute at Harvard University\nLocation: Cambridge\, Massachusett
 s\, USA (Harvard main campus)\nAward: Salary from $89\,999 per year with f
 ull Harvard benefits\; yearly operating budget from $225\,000 for lab supp
 lies\, travel and hiring personnel (postdoctoral fellows\, postbacs\, unde
 rgraduates)\; generous start-up funding for capital equipment based on the
  research programme\; dedicated laboratory space plus ancillary spaces (e.
 g. tissue culture)\; full principal investigator rights\; staff scientist 
 and engineer support for designing and fabricating experimental setups\; s
 hared research equipment across Harvard (Center for Nanoscale Systems\, Ba
 uer Life Science Core Facility)\; mentoring on lab culture\, scientific wr
 iting\, budgeting and leadership\; and access to Harvard's Core for Mentor
 ship Excellence. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to five years\, with fl
 exible start (incoming 2027 fellows start July to December 2027)\n\nMore: 
 https://www.rowland.harvard.edu/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.rowland.harvard.edu/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, USA (Harvard main campus)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:neh-public-humanities-projects-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260801T202838Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEH: Public Humanities Projects (2026-27 deadlines)
DESCRIPTION:NEH project grants supporting interpretive exhibitions\, histor
 ic-place programming and discussion programmes that bring humanities schol
 arship to public audiences. Aimed at strengthening the humanities in publi
 c life through scholarship-informed public engagement (rather than primary
  research). Two upcoming application windows: 15 October 2026 and 9 Decemb
 er 2026. Two scales: Planning grants up to USD 75\,000\, and Implementatio
 n grants up to USD 1\,000\,000 (most awards substantially smaller than the
  cap). ELIGIBILITY: US-incorporated non-profits with appropriate IRS statu
 s\, libraries\, museums\, historical organisations\, or accredited US coll
 eges and universities\; individual scholars participate as project directo
 rs on behalf of an eligible institution. Aimed at projects that engage bro
 ad public audiences with humanities content (history\, culture\, philosoph
 y\, literature\, etc.).\n\nOrganisation: National Endowment for the Humani
 ties (NEH)\nLocation: United States (applicants must be eligible US non-pr
 ofits - museums\, libraries\, historical organisations\, or accredited US 
 colleges and universities)\nAward: Planning grants up to USD 75\,000\; Imp
 lementation grants up to USD 1\,000\,000 (most awards substantially smalle
 r). Two upcoming deadlines: 15 October 2026 and 9 December 2026.\nDuration
 : Project-based\n\nMore: https://www.neh.gov/grants/public/public-humaniti
 es-projects?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.neh.gov/grants/public/public-humanities-projects?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (applicants must be eligible US non-profits - museum
 s\, libraries\, historical organisations\, or accredited US colleges and u
 niversities)
END:VEVENT
